WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.045 --> 00:00:01.920
INTRO:
Over the past few decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:07.600
bonds have fallen out of favor. The average&nbsp;
person isn’t even aware of what exactly bonds are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.800
while investors avoid them like the plague.&nbsp;
This is quite ironic given that the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:18.080
companies in the world often do the exact opposite&nbsp;
holding tens of billions if hundreds of billions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:24.720
in bonds. For example, we often hear that Apple&nbsp;
has nearly $200 billion in cash on hand, but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.840
is rather misleading because they don’t actually&nbsp;
hold anywhere near this amount in cash. Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.280
actually only holds $28 billion in cash. The&nbsp;
rest of Apple’s money is held in what are called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.680
marketable securities which are basically&nbsp;
just bonds and other fixed income assets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.200
Apple currently has $23 billion in short&nbsp;
term marketable securities and $141&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.720
billion in long term marketable securities. In&nbsp;
fact, a few years ago, Apple was the 23rd largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.000
US treasury investor in the world beating out most&nbsp;
countries. And if you take a look at the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.800
leading companies in the world, you’ll find a very&nbsp;
similar story. Google, for instance is currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.640
holding $113 billion in marketable securities. So,&nbsp;
here’s why these companies hold the vast majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.840
of their cash in marketable securities and why all&nbsp;
Americans should be investing in bonds as well.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.800
BONDS EXPLAINED:&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.840
Before we jump into why bonds could be a favorable&nbsp;
investment, let’s first recap what exactly bonds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.800
even are in the first place. Bonds are basically&nbsp;
IOU statements handed out by governments and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.600
corporations around the world. Whenever these&nbsp;
entities need money to build a road or a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.840
factory, they’ll issue these IOU statements&nbsp;
and they’ll promise to pay a certain amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.800
interest rate until they pay back the full amount.&nbsp;
So, you as the bond holder are basically a loaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.760
agency to these massive entities. Usually, the&nbsp;
vast majority of bonds are sold to institutions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:51.440
like pension funds and other countries like&nbsp;
China, but this isn’t on purpose. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.440
governments around the world would prefer to&nbsp;
issue their bonds to their citizens. This way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.640
they’re paying interest to their citizens instead&nbsp;
of China or Russia. Most governments actually go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.560
out of there way to ensure that their bonds&nbsp;
are easily accessible to the average person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.440
The US government, for example, guarantees that&nbsp;
they’ll offer you atleast $5 million worth of each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.480
bond every single time they issue bonds. In other&nbsp;
words, you don’t have to compete with the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.560
institutions and countries unless you’re buying&nbsp;
more than $5 million worth of treasury bonds on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.560
a regular basis. It’s not just the federal&nbsp;
governments that are issuing bonds either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.240
Usually, most areas will issue their own bonds&nbsp;
to fund a local dam or highway. These are called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.640
municipal bonds and they’re a great way to earn&nbsp;
money while supporting your local communities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.560
Also, interest payments earned from municipal&nbsp;
bonds are exempt from federal income taxes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.600
sometimes also state and local taxes as&nbsp;
well. And if you live in a high tax area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.520
this could make the interest earned from&nbsp;
these bonds far more valuable. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.240
if you’re taxed 40%, a municipal bond that pays&nbsp;
back 3% would actually be equivalent to a regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.440
investment paying back 5%. Anyway, moving onto&nbsp;
corporate bonds, this is where you’ll face the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.280
most amount of competition because companies&nbsp;
generally offer the highest interest rates and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.520
they issue a limited number of bonds. But if&nbsp;
you stick with large corporations like Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.160
who regularly issue billions bonds, it shouldn’t&nbsp;
be too hard to get your hands on these either.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.240
SECURITY:&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.640
Alright, now that we’ve discussed the basic&nbsp;
premise of bonds, let’s take a look at why they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.440
worth owning. By far the biggest advantage&nbsp;
of bonds and especially US treasury bonds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.640
is their safety. In fact, treasury bonds are so&nbsp;
safe that the interest earned from these bonds are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.680
considered risk free. I mean, this shouldn’t be&nbsp;
that surprising given that a country like the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:36.480
has virtually no risk of default because they can&nbsp;
just print money to pay back their bond holders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.120
This means that you’re basically guaranteed to&nbsp;
make money if you simply hold till maturity. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.080
this is why marketable securities are generally&nbsp;
just considered cash. Now, if you try to exit your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.240
investment early, you may be looking at a loss&nbsp;
depending on how the bond market is doing at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:54.720
given time. But, if you hold till maturity, it’s&nbsp;
basically like earning interest from a savings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.440
account which is unfortunately non existent&nbsp;
nowadays. Corporate bonds on the other hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:05.040
are by no means risk free, but one positive is&nbsp;
that their risk is always clearly defined. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.640
can look at a companies annual earnings, their&nbsp;
profit margins, and their balance sheet to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.960
a pretty good idea of how likely they are to&nbsp;
pay back their debt. And, if you don’t want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.320
do that, you can take a look at risk ratings from&nbsp;
reputable companies like S&amp;P or Moody’s. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.840
these ratings separate corporate bonds into&nbsp;
two sections which are investment-grade bonds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.640
and non-investment grade bonds. For our purposes,&nbsp;
we’re mainly going to focus on investment grade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:32.800
bonds. These ratings are super stringent and AAA&nbsp;
ratings are basically never handed out. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.920
there’s only 3 corporations in the entire world&nbsp;
that have a AAA rating which are Apple, Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:44.080
and Johnson &amp; Johnson. Also, Apple didn’t&nbsp;
even join this list till the end of last year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.440
Something else to note is that if these&nbsp;
companies due collapse for some reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.080
you’re one of the first people who are paid out&nbsp;
after liquidation. According to section 507 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.640
the bankruptcy code, the first people who are paid&nbsp;
out are secured debt holders who have collateral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.720
But, after these guys are paid out, bond&nbsp;
holders are next in line. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.440
shareholders are last in line and they’re&nbsp;
usually not paid very much if anything. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.160
if you’re looking for safety, stability, and&nbsp;
security, bonds are definitely the way to go.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:14.400
YIELD:&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.960
One of the biggest criticisms against bonds is&nbsp;
that their yields aren’t that high nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:25.200
but I don’t this is exactly true. Sure, bond&nbsp;
yields will never be as high as stock returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.840
but that’s because you’re taking on substantially&nbsp;
more risk and volatility by investing in stocks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.400
All assets are directly correlated to the risk&nbsp;
they carry, so it’s not fair to say that bonds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.520
as a whole are not worth it simply because they&nbsp;
offer lower returns than stocks. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:43.360
there’s plenty of bonds that offer great&nbsp;
yields if you do your research. Likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.840
the most underrated bond in the entire&nbsp;
world is I-series bonds. I-series bonds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.200
are inflation protected bonds that are&nbsp;
issued by the US government, and they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.360
specifically designed to ensure that you make&nbsp;
a return even after accounting for inflation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:01.120
And given that inflation is sky high right now,&nbsp;
so are yields on I Bonds. Right now, I Bonds are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.400
paying out a whopping 9.62% and you have up&nbsp;
until October to buy at these interest rates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.480
If you didn’t notice, that’s literally higher than&nbsp;
the average return of the S&amp;P 500. Now of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:17.040
if inflation goes down, so will the returns&nbsp;
on I-bonds, but you’ll never be losing money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:22.400
to inflation. In other words, this is a prefect&nbsp;
store of value that’s backed by the US government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.520
The only catch is that you can only buy $10,000&nbsp;
worth of I-bonds every calendar year. But, for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:32.080
average person, if you’re also maxing out your&nbsp;
401k and Roth IRA every year and building up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:37.280
larger emergency fund, like any responsible person&nbsp;
should do, this limit is likely more than enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.160
Also, if you do want to buy more bonds, there’s&nbsp;
plenty of solid options if you do some digging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:47.280
Take this Oracle bond for instance. This bond&nbsp;
matures in May of 2025, and at current prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:52.880
you can lock in a yield over 4%. And that’s just&nbsp;
for holding 3 years. If you’re willing lock in for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:58.480
a longer period like 20 years, you can buy this&nbsp;
2039 Oracle bond that currently offers a 5.79%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:03.760
yield. Some other high yielding investment&nbsp;
grade issuers include McDonald’s, AT&amp;T, Boeing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.400
and Fedex. So, clearly, if you look deep enough,&nbsp;
you can still find bonds that yield over 5%.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:09.040
USE CASES:&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:16.320
One of the biggest shortfalls&nbsp;
of most arguments against bonds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:21.200
is that they almost always compare bonds to&nbsp;
stocks or real estate. But, the flaw with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:26.400
argument is that bonds don’t have to be treated as&nbsp;
a replacement to stock or real estate investing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.840
Rather, they could be treated as a replacement to&nbsp;
cash. After all, basically every major corporation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:36.960
out there holds the vast majority of their cash in&nbsp;
marketable securities. Also, regardless of how low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:41.840
bond yields go, they will always be higher than&nbsp;
the yields offered in savings accounts. Now, I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.480
not suggesting that people hoard cash just so they&nbsp;
can invest in bonds. The number 1 thing that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:51.600
average person should be doing with excess savings&nbsp;
is investing in S&amp;P 500 in my opinion. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:56.640
this doesn’t mean that there’s no room for bonds.&nbsp;
There’s plenty of use cases in which bonds make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:02.240
lot more sense than cash. One example is saving&nbsp;
up for a house. Right now, the median price of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:07.600
a house in the US is $428,000. Let’s say this is&nbsp;
your first house and you’re looking to buy right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.960
underneath the median at $400,000. To come up&nbsp;
with a 20% down payment, you would need $80,000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:17.120
There’s also closing costs, moving&nbsp;
costs, and new furniture expenses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:22.000
so let’s say you budget out $100,000 for this&nbsp;
new house. And to achieve this, you’re looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.120
to save $20,000 per year for 5 years. If you were&nbsp;
to simply leave this money in a savings account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:32.000
you might be able to earn 0.5% per year.&nbsp;
At this rate, you would earn about $1200&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.880
in interest by the time you buy the house. Now,&nbsp;
what if you invested in Boeing bonds instead?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:42.720
Boeing obviously isn’t going anywhere in the next&nbsp;
5 years, and you can earn about a 5% yield with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:48.400
this Boeing May 2027 bond. This yield will slowly&nbsp;
fall with time as we get closer to maturity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:52.880
So, while you may be locking in a 5% yield during&nbsp;
your first 12 months of saving, you’ll probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:57.920
only be locking in 1% during your last 12 months&nbsp;
of saving. To keep things simple, let’s just say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:03.440
that you average a 3% yield throughout the next 5&nbsp;
years. At this rate, you would earn $7,600 by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:07.600
time you’re ready to buy your house. That itself&nbsp;
could be enough to pay for your closing costs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:12.080
And this logic doesn’t just apply to saving for&nbsp;
a house either. It makes sense whenever you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:17.520
money on a set date or whenever you have excess&nbsp;
cash reserves. Maybe you have $50,000 set aside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:22.240
for a car, but you’re waiting for the next model&nbsp;
to release in 6 or 12 months. Putting this money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:27.200
in reliable bonds would be way better than just&nbsp;
holding cash. Clearly, while bonds may not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.600
a great substitute for stocks or real estate,&nbsp;
they can be a phenomenal substitute for cash.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:35.680
OPPOSITION EXPLAINED:&nbsp;

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.720
Despite all these benefits, many people including&nbsp;
even Warren Buffett claim that bonds aren’t that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:45.360
useful in the current market environment. But, the&nbsp;
thing to note about virtually everyone who claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:51.040
this is that they’re not comparable to the average&nbsp;
person. Warren Buffett has averaged 20.5% per year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:56.400
from his stock investments since 1965. So, he’s&nbsp;
obviously not going to be thrilled about a bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:01.600
that’s paying 5% per year. But, for the average&nbsp;
person who isn’t a stock market genius and may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:05.920
not even have any investments at all, bonds could&nbsp;
be a great alternative to just having all of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:11.440
money in a savings account. Also, bonds could&nbsp;
be a great introduction to investing in general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:16.000
The everyday person is usually intimated by the&nbsp;
stock market because earnings reports and balance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.640
sheets seem complex and hard to understand.&nbsp;
But, bonds are extremely straightforward. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:25.680
loan money to the government or the most reputable&nbsp;
companies in the world, and they pay you back with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.480
interest by a certain date. As long as you hold&nbsp;
till maturity, you don’t have to worry volatility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:35.200
or fluctuating interest rates or any of that.&nbsp;
And starting to earn money through bonds may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.200
exactly what the average person needs to make&nbsp;
the plunge into stocks and take investing as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:44.880
a whole more seriously. And that’s why I think&nbsp;
all Americans should be investing in bonds, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:50.480
that’s just what I think. Would you guys invest in&nbsp;
bonds? Comment that down below. Also, drop a like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:55.360
if you think I bonds are underrated. And of course&nbsp;
consider joining our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.960
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
